/publikationen.bibliothek.kit.edu/1000003169)\nciteturn9670view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://publikationen.bibliothek.kit.edu/1000003169\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 79\nL0: cite0†KIT†kit.edu | cite1†KIT-Bibliothek†www.bibliothek.kit.edu | cite2†Impressum†www.bibliothek.kit.edu | cite3†Datenschutz†www.bibliothek.kit.edu L1: \nL2: cite4†Suche im KITopen-Katalog L3: \nL4: #### cite5†Repository KITopen†www.bibliothek.kit.edu L5: \nL6: # Self-stabilizing ring networks on connected graphs\nL7: \nL8: Cramer, Curt; Fuhrmann, Thomas\nL9: ## Abstract:\nL10: Large networks require scalable routing. Traditionally, protocol\nL11: overhead is\nL12: reduced by introducing a hierarchy. This requires aggregation of\nL13: nearby nodes\nL14: under a common address prefix. In fixed networks, this is achieved\nL15: administratively, whereas in wireless ad-hoc networks, dynamic\nL16: assignments of\nL17: nodes to aggregation units are required.\nL18: As a result of the nodes commonly being assigned a random\nL19: network address, the\nL20: majority of proposed ad-hoc routing protocols discovers routes\nL21: between end\nL22: nodes by flooding, thus limiting the network size. Peer-to-peer\nL23: (P2P) overlay\nL24: networks offer scalable routing solutions by employing\nL25: virtualized address spaces,\nL26: yet assume an underlying routing protocol for end-to-end\nL27: connectivity.\nL28: We investigate a cross-layer approach to P2P routing, where the\nL29: virtual address\nL30: space is implemented with a network-layer routing protocol by\nL31: itself. The Iterative\nL32: Successor Pointer Rewiring Protocol (ISPRP) efficiently\nL33: initializes a ring-structured\nL34: network among nodes having but link-layer connectivity. It is\nL35: fully self-organizing\nL36: and issues only a small per-node amount of messages by keeping\nL37: interactions\nL38: between nodes as local as possible.\nL39: ... mehr\nL40: The main contribution of this paper is a proof that ISPRP is\nL41: self-stabilizing, that is,\nL42: starting from an arbitrary initial state, the protocol lets the\nL43: network converge into\nL44: a correct state within a bounded amount of time.\nL45: cite6†Image: Open Access Logo†dbkit.bibliothek.kit.edu L46: \nL47: cite7†Volltext cite8†§ L48: \nL49: DOI: 10.5445/IR/1000003169\nL50: \nL51: Exportieren als ...\nL52: \nL53: cite9†BibTeX (UTF-8) cite10†BibTeX (ASCII) cite11†EndNote/Refer (.enw) cite12†RIS cite13†CSL-JSON cite14†ISI L54: \nL55: Seitenaufrufe: 211\nL56: seit 25.09.2018\nL57: \nL58: Downloads: 1138\nL59: seit 09.01.2009\nL60: \nL61: cite15†Image: Cover der Publikation†dbkit.bibliothek.kit.edu L62: \nL63: auf\nL64: \nL65: [Input]\nL66: Zugehörige Institution(en) am KIT  | Institut für Telematik (TM)\nL67: Publikationstyp  | Forschungsbericht/Preprint\nL68: Publikationsjahr  | 2005\nL69: Sprache  | Deutsch\nL70: Identifikator  | ISSN: 1432-7864\nL71: urn:nbn:de:swb:90-31697\nL72: KITopen-ID: 1000003169\nL73: Verlag  | Universität Karlsruhe (TH)\nL74: Serie  | Interner Bericht. Fakultät für Informatik, Universität Karlsruhe ; 2005,5\nL75: \nL76: KIT – Die Universität in der Helmholtz-Gemeinschaft\nL77: \nL78: KITopen Landing Page\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npv4.dvi (https://techrep.csc.ncsu.edu/2005/TR-2005-25.pdf)\nciteturn9670view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://techrep.csc.ncsu.edu/2005/TR-2005-25.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 675\nL0@P0: Self-Stabilizing Structured Ring Topology P2P Systems\nL1@P0: Ayman Shaker and Douglas S. Reeves\nL2@P0: Departments of Electrical and Computer Engineering and Computer Science\nL3@P0: N. C. State University\nL4@P0: {ashaker,reeves}@ncsu.edu\nL5@P0: Abstract\nL6@P0: We propose a self-stabilizing and modeless peer-to\u0002peer(P2P) network construction and maintenance protocol,\nL7@P0: called the Ring Network(RN) protocol. The RN protocol, when\nL8@P0: started on a network of peers that are in an arbitrary state, will\nL9@P0: cause the network to converge to a structured P2P system with\nL10@P0: a directed ring topology, where peers are ordered according to\nL11@P0: their identifiers. Furthermore, the RN protocol maintains this\nL12@P0: structure in the face of peer joins and departures.\nL13@P0: The RN protocol is a distributed and asynchronous\nL14@P0: message-passing protocol, which fits well the autonomous be\u0002havior of peers in a P2P system. The RN protocol requires\nL15@P0: only the existence of a bootstrapping system which is weakly\nL16@P0: connected. Peers do not need to be informed of any global\nL17@P0: network state, nor do they need to assist in repairing the net\u0002work topology when they leave. We provide a proof of the self\u0002stabilizing nature of the protocol, and experimentally measure\nL18@P0: the average cost (in time and number of messages) to achieve\nL19@P0: convergence.\nL20@P0: 1 Introduction\nL21@P0: P2P systems are decentralized and have several attrac\u0002tive properties like fault tolerance, load-balancing and self\u0002scaling[3]. P2P systems construct and maintain overlay net\u0002works with different network topologies, depending on appli\u0002cation requirements. Structured P2P systems impose specific\nL22@P0: local relationships between the peers, which gives rise to a\nL23@P0: global structure. The advantage of this structure is that it can\nL24@P0: then be used by the P2P system for efficient data placement,\nL25@P0: search, and retrieval.\nL26@P0: Current protocols for the construction and maintenance of\nL27@P0: specific structures generally rely on simplifying assumptions\nL28@P0: that are either undesirable or unrealistic. First, most such sys\u0002tems require or assume a specific initial configuration of the\nL29@P0: peers. Second, the construction and maintenance protocols as\u0002sume that the network remains in an ideal or close to ideal\nL30@P0: topology at all times. If the topology of the network deviates\nL31@P0: from this ideal, then these construction and maintenance pro\u0002tocols may cease to function correctly. Third, a dynamic net\u0002work, in the sense that peers arrive and depart continuously,\nL32@P0: can become partitioned. If this happens, the construction and\nL33@P0: maintenance protocols generally have no way to bring the net\u0002work back to the desired topology. One result of these limita\u0002tions is the difficulty of merging two structured P2P networks\nL34@P0: efficiently and smoothly. Existing construction and mainte\u0002nance protocols generally do not allow such merging, even if\nL35@P0: each network individually has the desired structure.\nL36@P0: In this paper we propose the Ring Network(RN) protocol;\nL37@P0: a distributed construction and maintenance protocol that gives\nL38@P0: rise to a sorted Ring Network, starting from an arbitrary topol\u0002ogy. This protocol is able to correctly merge two P2P networks\nL39@P0: into a single ring, or fix a network that is somehow partitioned.\nL40@P0: Furthermore, the protocol does not require the manual creation\nL41@P0: of a “seed” network, unlike most structured P2P systems. The\nL42@P0: protocol also does not require peers to be informed of or mon\u0002itor the global state of the network, before deciding what up\u0002dates to perform. Each peer simply runs the proposed con\u0002struction and maintenance protocols, without specific knowl\u0002edge of the current state of the network topology, and indepen\u0002dently of other peers.\nL43@P0: Existing methods of constructing structured P2P overlay\nL44@P0: networks generally adopt a top-down view of topology con\u0002struction and maintenance. These methods may require cen\u0002tral coordination, may have difficulty keeping up with the rate\nL45@P0: of change of typical P2P systems, and/or may not tolerate or\nL46@P0: recover from errors or failures. Efforts to contain or eliminate\nL47@P0: these problems sometimes lead to complex protocol “fixes”.\nL48@P0: Our method, in contrast, may be thought of as a bottom-up\nL49@P0: approach to constructing structured networks, in a way that is\nL50@P0: simple, robust, and fully distributed.\nL51@P0: The next section presents the network model and a formal\nL52@P0: definition of the problem. Section 3 is a review of related work.\nL53@P0: Section 4 describes the proposed protocol. Section 5 evaluates\nL54@P0: the correctness and the performance of the protocol. The paper\nL55@P0: is concluded in section 6.\nL56@P0: 2 Problem Statement\nL57@P0: A P2P network is a logical overlay network formed on top\nL58@P0-1: of a fixed infrastructure network, such as the Internet. There\u0002fore, any peer u in the network can communicate or send amessage to any other peer v directly, if u knows the network\nL59@P1: address of v. In such a case, v is said to be a neighbor of u.\nL60@P1: A P2P network topology is defined by the set of peers in a\nL61@P1: network, and the neighbor relationships between those peers.\nL62@P1: This topology can be represented by a graph, with vertexes\nL63@P1: representing peers, and a directed edge from vertex u to vertex\nL64@P1: v if v is a neighbor of u. Let the neighbors of peer u be denoted\nL65@P1: as u.Γ. 1 We further denote the ith neighbor of peer u by u.Γi.\nL66@P1: The 0th neighbor, u.Γ0, is a special neighbor of u and is also\nL67@P1: called the successor of u. If v is the successor of u, then u is a\nL68@P1: predecessor of v. If v is the ith neighbor of u, v = u.Γi, then\nL69@P1: let u.index(v)= i.\nL70@P1: Each peer has a unique identifier or ID, in the form of a\nL71@P1: nonnegative integer. Let the largest possible ID assignable to\nL72@P1: a peer be m − 1. We assume each peer knows the value of m.\nL73@P1: The distance from a peer u to a peer v is defined by a distance\nL74@P1: function, dm(u, v)= v − u, where subtraction is performed\nL75@P1: modulo m.\nL76@P1: 2 For simplicity of presentation, we omit the sub\u0002script m from the distance function in this paper. The smaller\nL77@P1: the value of d(u, v) the closer v is said to be to u. For the\nL78@P1: purposes of this paper we define a P2P network as being a ring\nL79@P1: network if for each peer u in the network, u’s successor, v, is\nL80@P1: the peer closest to u from the set of all peers and v \u0002= u (unless\nL81@P1: the system consists of a single peer, in which case the succes\u0002sor of u will be itself). Note that the ring network is directed.\nL82@P1: If v is the neighbor of u, then the opposite is not necessarily\nL83@P1: true.\nL84@P1: We refer to a peer v as being between a peer u and a peer\nL85@P1: w if v is closer to u than w is, or if v = w. As an alternative\nL86@P1: representation, we will write u<v ≤ w when v is between\nL87@P1: u and w. This definition is not dependent on the topology of\nL88@P1: the network; it is simply the relative ordering of three peers,\nL89@P1: according to their identifiers.\nL90@P1: To fully define a P2P system, one has to take into consider\u0002ation bootstrapping, which is the method by which peers are\nL91@P1: initially made aware of other peers in the system. Let the peers\nL92@P1: known to the bootstrapping system, and whose addresses are\nL93@P1: returned by the bootstrapping system in response to queries, be\nL94@P1: termed the bootstrapping peers. The bootstrapping peers and\nL95@P1: the neighbor connections between them must form a weakly\nL96@P1: connected graph. A weakly connected graph is a directed\nL97@P1: graph in which there is a path between any two vertexes, ig\u0002noring the directionality of the edges. In a strongly connected\nL98@P1: graph, there is a path between any two vertexes, considering\nL99@P1: directionality of the edges. All strongly connected graphs are\nL100@P1: also weakly connected, but the reverse is not true. We now\nL101@P1: prove that weakly connected bootstrapping systems are nec\u0002essary and sufficient to ensure that all peers will be able to\nL102@P1: communicate once they join together in a P2P network topol\u0002ogy.\nL103@P1: Proposition 2.1. For all peers to be able to communicate by\nL104@P1: means of the topology created by a P2P system, if each peer\nL105@P1: 1When referencing a variable v stored by peer u, we will write u.v. 2The distance function is unidirectional. The distance from v to u is not\nL106@P1: necessarily the same as the distance from u to v.\nL107@P1: only queries the bootstrapping system once it is necessary for\nL108@P1: the bootstrapping system to be weakly connected.\nL109@P1: Proof. If the bootstrapping system is not weakly connected,\nL110@P1: there there will be at least two peers u and v for which there\nL111@P1: is neither a path from u to v, nor a path from v to u. It is\nL112@P1: therefore impossible for u and v to communicate with each\nL113@P1: other through any sequence of neighbors.\nL114@P1: Proposition 2.2. For all peers to be able to communicate by\nL115@P1: means of the topology created by a P2P system, it is a sufficient\nL116@P1: condition for the bootstrapping system to be weakly connected.\nL117@P1: Proof. Assume that the every peer is connected to at least one\nL118@P1: bootstrapping peer, and the bootstrapping peers are weakly\nL119@P1: connected. If every peer u then notifies each of its neighbors v\nL120@P1: of its address, and v forms a connection to u, the resulting net\u0002work will be strongly connected. Therefore, every peer will be\nL121@P1: able to communicate with every other peer through some set\nL122@P1: of peers.\nL123@P1: Therefore, a weakly connected bootstrapping system is the\nL124@P1: simplest and most general form of bootstrapping. We call such\nL125@P1: a bootstrapping system a minimal bootstrapping system. En\u0002suring the bootstrapping system is weakly connected in gen\u0002eral is not difficult. For instance, connecting the bootstrapping\nL126@P1: peers together in an unordered chain will make them weakly\nL127@P1: connected. In some previous works, the bootstrapping system\nL128@P1: is replaced by a seed network. Most seed networks are required\nL129@P1: to have a minimum size and/or a specific structure, and this\nL130@P1: structure may be highly constrained. In fact, the seed network\nL131@P1: may need to be an instance of the target topology which the\nL132@P1: construction protocol seeks to create and maintain. In contrast,\nL133@P1: a minimal bootstrapping system imposes no such requirement,\nL134@P1: and is appropriate for constructing arbitrary topologies.\nL135@P1: We next give the definition of a self-stabilizing P2P sys\u0002tem. We define a self-stabilizing P2P protocol as one which\nL136@P1: can give rise to a desired topology starting from the simplest\nL137@P1: of necessary and sufficient assumptions.\nL138@P1: Definition 2.3 (Self-Stabilizing P2P system). A P2P system\nL139@P1: that constructs a desired topology, starting with each non\u0002bootstrapping peer having an arbitrary set of neighbors (in\u0002cluding no neighbors). To achieve this, the P2P system relies\nL140@P1: only on the existence of a minimal bootstrapping system.\nL141@P1: Some P2P systems may function when the bootstrapping\nL142@P1: system is minimal, but impose requirements on the initial\nL143@P1: neighbor relationships of non-bootstrapping peers. Such P2P\nL144@P1: systems will not be self-stabilizing, because they depend on\nL145@P1: a specific initial topology. In addition, a P2P system that as\u0002sumes the bootstrapping system is strongly connected is not a\nL146@P1: self-stabilizing P2P system.\nL147@P1: In this paper, we assume an asynchronous, reliable\nL148@P1: message-passing model. This means a message sent from peer\nL149@P1: u to peer v is eventually delivered in a finite but unbounded\nL150@P1: time. Furthermore, we assume that messages sent between\nL151@P1-2: two peers in the same direction follow the FIFO rule, as wouldbe the case over a single TCP connection. We allow peers\nL152@P2: to execute steps of the protocol in an asynchronous fashion.\nL153@P2: A synchronous protocol would assume protocol execution oc\u0002curs at the same rate (or even at the same time) on all peers,\nL154@P2: and that message transmission time is bounded. In many cases,\nL155@P2: this simplifies the analysis of the protocol. On the other hand,\nL156@P2: asynchronous protocols assume only that transmission time is\nL157@P2: finite, and do not make any assumption about the relative speed\nL158@P2: of protocol execution on different peers. This leads to the fol\u0002lowing definition:\nL159@P2: Definition 2.4 (Distributed Asynchronous P2P system). A\nL160@P2: P2P system in which the form of communication between peers\nL161@P2: is message-passing, where message transmission time is finite\nL162@P2: but unbounded. Furthermore, there is no shared memory or\nL163@P2: shared clock(s) between peers. All peers execute the same pro\u0002tocol.\nL164@P2: In our network model peer failures and departures follow\nL165@P2: the fail-stop model as specified in [14]: peers are able to per\u0002fectly detect the failure or departure of other peers in the P2P\nL166@P2: network. We define a modeless P2P system as follows:\nL167@P2: Definition 2.5 (Modeless P2P protocol). A modeless P2P\nL168@P2: protocol is one in which it is not required for every peer to\nL169@P2: be notified of each peer arrival or departure, or to be aware of\nL170@P2: the total number of peers.\nL171@P2: Modeless P2P protocols are highly desirable. They avoid\nL172@P2: the need for global communication among peers, or the need\nL173@P2: for periodic protocol synchronization, and are therefore faster\nL174@P2: and have lower overhead.\nL175@P2: We now define our problem with reference to above proper\u0002ties: Given are n peers, each with an arbitrary set of neighbors\nL176@P2: (or no neighbors), and a minimal bootstrapping process. De\u0002sign a modeless and distributed asynchronous P2P protocol\nL177@P2: that is self-stabilizing, and that results in the construction of a\nL178@P2: ring network.\nL179@P2: 3 Related Work\nL180@P2: The problem of organizing nodes in a distributed system\nL181@P2: into a desired topology has been approached in many ways.\nL182@P2: This review of related work focuses on two properties: (i)\nL183@P2: whether the methods are self-stabilizing, and (ii) whether the\nL184@P2: protocols are modeless or not.\nL185@P2: P2P research has addressed the organization of peers into\nL186@P2: a desired topological structure. Many structured P2P systems\nL187@P2: are based on distributed hash tables (DHTs) [9]. One of the\nL188@P2: most widely-cited such P2P system is Chord [17]. In [14],\nL189@P2: Liben-Nowell et al. describe maintenance protocols for Chord\nL190@P2: that may be run by each peer in order to keep the topology\nL191@P2: from deviating substantially from a chordal ring. The Chord\nL192@P2: protocols, however, always assume that the topology has some\nL193@P2: partial structure. In the absence of such minimal structure, the\nL194@P2: Chord protocols cease to guarantee convergence to the correct\nL195@P2: topology. Therefore, they do not qualify as self-stabilizing.\nL196@P2: In [1], Angluin et al. solve the problem identified in this\nL197@P2: paper, i.e., starting from a weakly connected network, build\nL198@P2: an ordered ring network. Though the protocols give rise to\nL199@P2: a structured P2P network with low complexity, their solution\nL200@P2: does not address failures or joins of peers. Furthermore, all\nL201@P2: peers are required to start execution of the protocols at the\nL202@P2: same time. Therefore, although the protocol presented in this\nL203@P2: work is self-stabilizing, it is neither asynchronous nor mode\u0002less.\nL204@P2: Topology construction using gossip-based protocols has\nL205@P2: been introduced in the T-Man protocol [7]. In this work, peers\nL206@P2: are organized according to a ranking function to produce a de\u0002sired network topology, such as ring or torus topologies. T\u0002man assumes that the network starts out with a topology close\nL207@P2: to a random graph, which is a stronger requirement than a\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2007 Proceedings of the Workshop on Algorithm Engineering and Experiments (ALENEX) | Linearization: Locally Self-Stabilizing Sorting in Graphs (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611972870.10)\nciteturn9670view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611972870.10\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 388\n"
